[Event "Scholars mate: the four move mate"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/TQupggWr/BJcLQp6o"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Today you learn scholars mate. Four moves, and the game is over. Open in the middle. [%cal Ye2e4] }
1. e4 { That one little pawn opens two doors at once: one for your queen, one for your bishop. [%csl Gd1,Gf1] } (1. d4 { A fine opening, and a whole different world. But no queen road, no quick mate. } 1... d5 2. c4 { The queens gambit. Lovely chess, and nobody gets mated in four. }) (1. Nf3 { A good move. But look where the knight sat down: right in your own queens doorway. [%csl Rf3] } 1... d5 2. d4 { Solid chess, and the four move mate is off the menu. }) 1... e5 { He copies you. Now send the bishop out to stare straight at the red square. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yf1c4] } 2. Bc4 { Your bishop stares straight down the diagonal at the red square, and only his king guards it. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gc4f7] } (2. Qh5 { Queen first works too. But out in the open, everybody gets to poke at her. } 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 { The same picture, one move later. }) (2. Nf3 { The Italian is coming, and it is a fine opening. But the knight just blocked your queen. [%csl Rf3] } 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 { Good chess. The mate road is shut, though. }) (2. Qf3 { She eyes the red square from here as well, but his knight has a door it can slam. [%csl Rf7] } 2... Nf6 { Blocked already. His knight drops into the doorway, and the sneaky queen road was the other one. [%csl Gf6] }) 2... Nc6 { He guards his pawn and never sees it coming. Now fly your queen out to join the attack. [%cal Yd1h5] } 3. Qh5 { Two attackers on the red square now, and one guard. From out here no knight can get in her way. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gh5f7,Gc4f7] } (3. Qf3 { Your queen stares at the red square from here too, but he still has a door to slam. [%csl Rf7] } 3... Nf6 { The knight steps into the doorway and the mate is gone. [%csl Gf6] }) (3. Nf3 { Sensible chess, and now you are simply playing the Italian. } 3... Bc5 { A good game for both. But the queen never got out. }) (3. Bxf7+ { The bishop cannot do this on his own. He simply takes it. } 3... Kxf7 { A whole bishop for one pawn, and his king strolls away. [%csl Rf7] }) (3. Nc3 { A normal, healthy developing move, and it does nothing at all about the red square. [%csl Rf7] } 3... Nf6 { Solid chess for both. The trap needed your queen to fly out first. [%cal Gd1h5] }) (3. d3 { Quiet and sensible, and your queen is still sitting at home. } 3... Nf6 { A perfectly good game, and nothing like a four move mate. }) 3... Nf6 { And there it is. The most natural move on the board, and it loses on the spot: only a pawn poke at your queen could have saved him. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yh5f7,Gc4f7] } 4. Qxf7# { Mate! Your queen lands right beside his king and your bishop guards her. That is scholars mate. [%csl Gf7][%cal Gc4f7] } (4. Bxf7+ { Check! And it throws the mate away, because his knight is glaring at your queen. [%cal Rf6h5] } 4... Ke7 5. Qd1 Kxf7 { Your queen ran home and he ate the bishop. All that for nothing. }) (4. Qxe5+ { A check and a free pawn. But look who was guarding that square. [%cal Rc6e5] } 4... Nxe5 { Your queen for one pawn, and the mate that was there is gone. }) 1-0


[Event "Scholars mate: how black stops it"]
[Result "0-1"]
