[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/2B1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK1NR w KQkq - 2 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/2B1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK1NR_w_KQkq_-_2_3"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Now you are black, and one day someone will try that on you. Do not panic. }
3. Qh5 { Out comes his queen, and she and the bishop both want the red square. Shoo her away with the g pawn. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yg7g6] } 3... g6 { One small pawn, and it both blocks her road and pokes at her. She must move again. [%cal Gg6h5] } (3... Nf6 { The most natural move on the board, and it walks straight into mate. } 4. Qxf7# { The queen lands, the bishop guards her, and your king has nowhere to go. }) (3... d6 { That looks solid, and it does nothing at all about the red square. [%csl Rf7] } 4. Qxf7# { Same mate. Guarding the middle does not guard your front door. }) (3... Qe7 { Good defending. Your queen covers the red square and the mate is dead. [%csl Rf7][%cal Ge7f7] } 4. d3 Nf6 { Perfectly fine. But your queen is standing in your own bishops way. }) (3... Qf6 { Your queen guards the red square too, but she has taken the square your knight wanted. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gf6f7] } 4. Nc3 Bc5 { Playable, and a little clumsy. The pawn move is the tidy one. }) (3... Nh6 { That knight covers the red square, and the poor thing is stuck out on the rim. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gh6f7] } 4. Nc3 g6 { You play the pawn move anyway, with a sadder knight. }) (3... d5 { It hits the bishop, and it costs a pawn and then a knight. } 4. Bxd5 Qf6 5. Bxc6+ bxc6 { He is a pawn up and your queen side is a wreck. Not worth it. }) 4. Qf3 { His queen slides across and stares at the red square all over again. Now block the road with a knight. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yg8f6] } 4... Nf6 { Your knight slams the door on the green square. He moved his queen twice, you developed twice. You are better. [%csl Gf6] } (4... Bg7 { A lovely square for the bishop, and the road to the red square is still wide open. [%csl Rf7][%cal Rf3f7] } 5. Qxf7# { Mate. The knight had to block first. The bishop could have waited. }) (4... d6 { Solid looking, and that pawn does not block her road at all. } 5. Qxf7# { Mate again. She came straight down the column your bishop sits on, so block that column. }) (4... Qe7 { Your queen guards the red square, and that is perfectly good too. [%csl Rf7][%cal Ge7f7] } 5. Ne2 Nf6 { Fine chess. The knight does the same job and stays out of the way. }) (4... Qf6 { Offering a queen trade. Your queen defends the red square, and she steals your knights best square. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gf6f7] } 5. Qxf6 Nxf6 { Queens off, danger over. Perfectly fine, and the pawn move was tidier. }) (4... Nh6 { It stops the mate, and the knight is miserable out on the rim. } 5. Ne2 Na5 { Playable. The other knight was the one that wanted the job. }) (4... d5 { It attacks the bishop, and he simply takes it and keeps the threat. } 5. Bxd5 Qd7 { A pawn gone, and you are still busy defending the red square. [%csl Rf7] }) 0-1


