{ The mate is dead. Now send him the bill, because his queen is still out there. }
5. Nc3 { He finally develops a piece. His queen is loose in the middle. Find the knight jump. [%csl Rf3] } 5... Nd4 { Beautiful. The knight lands in the middle and hits two things at once: his queen, and a free pawn. [%cal Gd4f3,Gd4c2] } (5... Bg7 { A good square, and it lets his queen stroll away for free. } 6. Nge2 d6 { A fine game. But you had a free hit on the queen and let it go. }) (5... d6 { Solid, and slow. The queen was the one you wanted to chase. } 6. Nd5 Bg7 { Playable, and now his knight is the annoying one. }) (5... Nb4 { The other jump. That knight pokes at a pawn and lets his queen stroll off the hook. [%cal Gb4c2] } 6. Bb3 { One step back, and his bishop now guards the pawn your knight is eyeing. That knight has no job. [%cal Gb3c2] }) 6. Qd1 { All the way home. Three queen moves, and she is back where she started. Now develop. } 6... Bg7 { Down the long diagonal. He spent three moves on his queen, you spent yours on pieces. [%cal Gg7a1] } (6... Nxc2+ { A free pawn, and a fork of king and rook! Except the queen just came home. } 7. Qxc2 { His queen guards that square from her home, and your whole knight is gone. [%cal Rd1c2] }) (6... c6 { This is just as good: the pawn takes the best square away from his knight. } 7. Nf3 Bg7 { Excellent. The bishop comes out anyway, one move later. }) (6... Bc5 { A good square too. But your own g pawn opened a road, and that bishop wants to sit on it. } 7. Nf3 c6 { Perfectly playable, just a little less to do with your plan. }) (6... d6 { Safe and slow. Your bishop was already asking for the road your g pawn opened. } 7. d3 { Fine for both. But your g pawn opened up the green square, and your bishop was asking for it. [%csl Gg7] }) 0-1
